/// A look at the machine, for the System monitor (P4 task 9): the processes with
/// their memory and CPU time, the CPUs and memory, and the model's state. Like
/// `kai:edit` and `kai:settings`, this is a granted interface, not part of
/// `kai:app` (which is frozen): only an app whose package holds the `system`
/// grant can import it, and a component that imports it without the grant does
/// not stage (`Reason::Link`). It adds no row for the AI and reads only; stopping
/// a process stays the OS's `process_stop`, an effect that asks.
///
/// `read` only queues. The host takes the queue after the call, reads the
/// kernel's `METRICS` and asks the llm service for its `health`, and tells the
/// app the answer in a later call, so a call never waits on the system, and a
/// version rehearsed beside the active one cannot read twice: only the shown
/// version's queue is taken. The answer is one sample in the `systemabi` format
/// (`KSY1`, little-endian numbers and length-prefixed names, at most 32 KiB);
/// the app parses it as it would any input, and a sample it cannot parse is a
/// reading it does not show.
package kai:system@0.1.0;

interface system {
    /// Asks for one sample and returns its ticket. Refused (the call traps) when
    /// 4 reads are waiting for the host already.
    read: func() -> u32;
}

/// What the system answered.
interface samples {
    /// The answer to the read with this ticket: one sample, or why there is none
    /// (the system could not be read, or the answer was too large; at most 200
    /// printable ASCII bytes). A ticket is not reused while this instance runs; a
    /// new instance starts again from 0.
    on-sample: func(ticket: u32, outcome: result<list<u8>, string>);
}

/// An app that reads the machine: the two halves of the grant.
world system-read {
    import system;
    export samples;
}
